# Execution plans

`docs/plans/` files exist so a fresh session can start one phase with nothing else.
The table near the top is the scan surface; each phase section is the whole prompt.

Every plan has this table near the top — after the "why this exists" paragraph, before the
first phase. The header is exactly this, and `pnpm plans:check` (CI) reads every cell, so the
table is a contract, not a style:

| Phase | What | After | Model | State |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | … | — | quick (low) | |
| 1 | … | 0 | workhorse (medium) | |
| 3 | … | 1, 2; enrollment | deep (xhigh) | |

- **Phase** — the id: a number with an optional letter prefix or suffix (`0`, `4a`, `R0a`).
  Always `Phase`, never `Stage` or `Step`. Words (`optional`, `was R4`) go in What.
- **After** — prerequisites. Prior phases by id, comma-separated, `1–5` for a range (`4` covers
  `4a`–`4c`); `—` if none. A non-phase gate (enrollment, a machine, a secret, "R4a deployed")
  goes after a `;`, one gate per `;`. When a gate clears, keep it and add the date:
  `0; enrollment (cleared 2026-09-19)`. The board reads "cleared" as open.
- **Model** — suggested tier for *that* phase (Model tiers, below), **with its thinking level in
  parentheses**: `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`. Spend the reasoning budget where a
  mistake is silent; cheap models for mechanical work with loud failures. A human
  phase is `— (human)` with no level.

  Tier and level are one decision, not two axes, which is why they share a cell —
  and they do not have to move together. A small build with a silent failure mode
  earns a high level on a cheap tier; a large mechanical diff that fails loudly does
  not. Say which case it is in the phase's own line.

  Restate it on the first line of each phase section as
  **`**Model:** <tier> · **Thinking:** <level> — <one-line why>`**. The table is the
  scan surface and stays terse; the phase line is the worker's prompt, so it spells
  both out and says *why* that level.

  **Always.** A model with no thinking level is an incomplete plan, not a default:
  a worker left to guess the level runs at whatever the session happens to be set to,
  and that is exactly how a silent-failure phase runs cheap. So:
  - Never write, add, or reassign a phase's model without its level, in the table cell
    *and* the phase line.
  - Before starting a phase whose level is missing, pick one, write it into the plan
    (both places, with the why), and only then start. This is the one retrofit that
    is always allowed — see the note at the end of this file.
  - Editing a plan's phase table for any reason (State, a model swap, a new phase)
    fills every missing level in that table while you are there.
- **State** — `<status>[ (#PR, …)][; note]…`. The status is one of:

  | Status | Means |
  |---|---|
  | *(blank)* | not started |
  | `running` | a worker is on it |
  | `QA passed (#N)` | draft PR passed QA, waiting on the user's click and a merge |
  | `landed (#N)` | merged into `dev` (the PR number may be missing on old phases) |
  | `blocked; <why>` | can't proceed until something outside the table changes |
  | `dropped; <why>` | won't be built; stays in the table for the record |

  Everything else is a note after a `;`: `landed (#186); 3 human checks open`,
  `landed (#231); in prod`. No other status words (`done`, `hard-gated`, `not planned`,
  `optional`, `running (review 2)`); the lint fails on them.

Each phase section is complete on its own: goal, files, tests, bar for done.

- **Every Done-when item carries its check**: `→ verify: <command, test, or thing to look at>`.
  An item nobody can check isn't done-able; rewrite it until it is. `pnpm plans:check` fails a
  phase still to run (not landed, dropped, or human) that has no `## Phase <id>` section, no
  `**Done when:**` block, or an item without `→ verify:`, and names a near miss (`-> verify`).
- **Unknown territory gets a Research block** before Files: what to read first and the open
  questions. The worker answers them *into the plan* (via the showrunner) before building, because
  a wrong line in a plan becomes hundreds of wrong lines of code.
- **Cite what already applies**: before writing a phase, `grep -ril <area> docs/learnings/
  docs/decisions/` and link the hits in the phase, so the worker doesn't re-learn them.
Start prompt: *"read `docs/plans/<file>.md` and do Phase N."*

**Handing off a new plan.** Whoever writes a plan ends that reply with two copy-paste prompts,
in code blocks, with the real path and the first phase's own id filled in (`0`, `1`, `R0a`):

````markdown
To start Phase 1, paste this into a fresh session:
```
read docs/plans/<file>.md and do Phase 1
```
Or build the whole plan, phase by phase, by pasting this:
```
read docs/plans/<file>.md and showrun it (showrunner.mdc)
```
````

Why: the user's next move is one of those two, and a ready-to-paste line saves them
reconstructing it from the rule.

**One feature branch per phase**, draft PR into `dev` (`branching.mdc`).

## Model tiers

Plans and rules name a tier, never a model, so they don't date and each project picks its own
models without touching a plan. `pnpm models` prints the table this project runs with and where
each cell came from (`scripts/model-tiers.test.mjs` fails on a model name in any other harness file).

| Tier | For |
|---|---|
| `quick` | Mechanical work that fails loudly. |
| `workhorse` | Most building: the default. |
| `deep` | Silent-failure, security, release; QA and orchestration. |
| `frontier` | The hardest reasoning, when `deep` isn't enough. |

- **`model-tiers.json`** (harness): whippletree's suggested model for each tier and lineage, with
  the id each tool takes. Updated in whippletree when a model ships; `harness:pull` brings it as a
  diff the project can take or leave.
- **`agent-overrides.json`** → `models` (project, optional): `{ "<tier>": { "<lineage>": … } }`,
  any cell; a person's gitignored `agent-overrides.local.json` wins over it (`docs/guide/overrides.md`). A model id pins it; `latest` follows the newest (home: the Claude Code family alias;
  rival and wildcard: the tool's own default); `none` drops the pick. An unknown tier, lineage, or
  empty value fails loudly.
- No pick for a cell: use the tier below.

Lineages are the model families, one column each:

- **`home`** (Anthropic, Claude Code): what runs unless a plan says otherwise.
- **`rival`** (OpenAI, Codex): a second opinion that doesn't share `home`'s blind spots. A pick on
  `deep` switches on the rival in the dynamite test; no pick there switches it off
  (`dynamite-test.mdc` → Rival).
- **`wildcard`** (xAI, Grok CLI): a third voice, to break a tie between the other two.

Setup, and how to find each tool's model ids: `docs/guide/models.md`. `pnpm models catalog` lists
every model each vendor offers with the tier whippletree suggests for it.

A plan names a lineage only when it isn't `home`, after the level: `deep (xhigh) · rival`.

## Human checks

Anything only a person can verify (a physical device, a real walk or reboot, another OS, a
judgment call, a check on prod after deploy) goes in the phase section as a `Human checks`
block, never only in prose. The wrangler compiles every block into one living checklist the
user ticks (`wrangler.mdc` → Human checks), and ticks come back to update State.

```markdown
**Human checks**
- `sync-p2-offline` · iPhone · 10 min · ios-release — Airplane mode, log two entries, reconnect: both appear on web.
- `sync-p3-walk-out` · iPhone · walk · ios-release · waits #42 — Walk out the door: the geofence rule fires once.
- `prod-still-signed-in` · prod · 2 min · deploy — After deploy, phone and web are still signed in.
```

- **id**: kebab-case, globally unique, prefixed with the plan's slug (`sync-`, `security-`,
  `desktop-`), `prod-` for post-deploy checks, or `ci-` for repo-wide calls (billing, CI budget).
  Never reuse or rename one: ticks are keyed on it.
- **where**: `prod`, `iPhone`, `Watch`, `Mac`, `Windows`, `Linux`, or `decision`. This picks
  the checklist station. The project's `docs/human-checks.json` lists its stations (each with
  its `where` values) and its gates; those are the only values the lint accepts. Without that
  file, the where and gate values listed here are the defaults.
- **time**: a rough duration (`2 min`, `~45 min`, `walk`).
- **gate**: what it blocks. `deploy` (check right after the ship lands), `ios-release`,
  `desktop-release`, or `none` for a judgment call.
- **waits** (optional): a PR or phase it can't start before.
- Then the check itself, as an instruction with the pass condition in it. Its first sentence
  becomes the checklist title, so lead with a short one.
- An **indented line** under an item is a note on it (e.g. `  **Approved 2026-09-19** (user)`).
  Any other non-item line ends the block.
- Checks that belong to no plan (post-deploy `prod-`, repo-wide `ci-`) live in
  `docs/human-checks.md`, in the same format. A runbook scenario's check lives in its runbook
  (`docs/runbooks/<area>.md`, `testing.mdc` → tier 3).
- `pnpm plans:check` parses every block in all three places, with the compiler's own parser
  (`scripts/lib/human-checks.mjs`), and fails on an unparsed item, a duplicate id, an unknown
  gate or where, a misspelled `**Human checks**` header, an item outside any block, a list item
  in a block that isn't a check, a block with no items, an unindented line right under a check,
  or a code fence or HTML comment that never closes. A block may sit indented inside a list; a
  line indented past a check is a note on it.

A phase whose human checks aren't all ticked says so in State (`landed; 3 human checks open`),
not `device acceptance pending`.

To run a whole plan with one session dispatching phases to worker agents, follow
`showrunner.mdc`.

Beyond the table, do not retrofit an existing plan's shape unless you are already rewriting
it. The table itself is never grandfathered: fixing it until `pnpm plans:check` passes is always
allowed. Landed phases may keep a model without a level (or `—` when none was recorded); every phase
still to run needs one.
The shape to copy: `docs/plans/_template.md`.

Only plans live in `docs/plans/`: every `.md` there except `_template.md` needs the table.
Reference material (a runbook, a seed list, a spike write-up) goes elsewhere in `docs/`.

## The board

`pnpm plans:board` (the `plan-board` skill) renders every plan's table as one page: a lane per
plan with a completion line, each phase colored by where it stands (landed, landed with checks
open, QA passed, running, ready to start, gated, blocked, waiting), and what to start next.
With `--gh` it also flags a State that disagrees with GitHub (a merged PR on a phase not
`landed`). The project's companion names the artifact it publishes to.
